{{smaller block|The Anglo-Saxons habitually wore upon their arms twisted bracelets or ''torques'', or, in their stead, a number of simple bracelets.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', VI. 465.}}}}

2. In ''mech.'', the moment of a system-force applied so as to twist anything, as a shaft in machinery.

{{smaller block|The ''torque'', or turning moment, is, in a series dynamo, both when used as a generator and when used as a motor, very nearly proportional to the current.
{{right|''S. P. Thompson'', Dynamo-Electric Machinery, p. 45.}}}}

'''torqued''' (tôrkt), ''a.'' [< OF. ''torquer'', twist, < L. ''torquere'', twist (see ''torque''), + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] 1. Twisted; convoluted.

{{smaller block|On this West shore we found a dead fish floating, which had in his nose a horne streight and ''torquet'', of length two yards lacking two ynches.
{{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', III. 35.}}}}

2. Twisted like a rope: said of metal-work.

